            <fr:meta name="toc">true</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    How a computation becomes algebra. Three formats dominate — <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002S/" title="R1CS" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002S/" display-uri="dhsorens-002S" type="local">R1CS</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002T/" title="Plonkish Arithmetisation" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002T/" display-uri="dhsorens-002T" type="local">Plonkish</fr:link>, and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002U/" title="AIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002U/" display-uri="dhsorens-002U" type="local">AIR</fr:link> — and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002V/" title="CCS and the Unified View" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002V/" display-uri="dhsorens-002V" type="local">CCS</fr:link> is the claim that they are one object with different sparsity patterns.
</html:p>
            <html:p><fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Y/" title="Clean: Circuits and Their Specifications in Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Y/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Y" type="local">Clean</fr:link> sits at this layer: an embedded DSL that writes circuits for these formats in <fr:link href="/dhsorens-lean/" title="The Lean Theorem Prover" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-lean/" display-uri="dhsorens-lean" type="local">Lean</fr:link>, with the specification and the soundness proof next to the circuit. The open question is what "this circuit implements this specification" should mean, and which arithmetisation makes that statement easiest to prove — the same question as for <fr:link href="/dhsorens-001J/" title="Smart Contract Specification" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-001J/" display-uri="dhsorens-001J" type="local">smart contract specifications</fr:link>, with better-behaved mathematics.
</html:p>
